    // We craft a test that calls multicall on C with arbitrary bytes[] payloads (encoded as two calls):
    // We will call multicall with two arbitrary delegatecall payloads (passed in by the caller as params).
    // The property: after multicall returns (or reverts) the invariant validated inside multicall
    // in CapsLibrary must hold. Since multicall performs the check and will revert if it fails,
    // we assert that if multicall did NOT revert then the post-state satisfies
    // borrowATokenSupplyAfter <= borrowATokenSupplyBefore + (debtTokenSupplyBefore - debtTokenSupplyAfter)
    // which rearranges to the same relation (i.e., the increase in borrowAToken supply is <= decrease in debt token supply)

    // Note: we can only observe via public getters: borrowAToken.totalSupply() and debtToken.totalSupply()
    // Those are accessible through c.data().borrowAToken.totalSupply() ? No: data() returns DataView with borrowAToken as NonTransferrableScaledToken
    // But we cannot access nested contract's methods from returned struct. Instead, SizeView exposes getPositionsCount and other views.
    // However borrowAToken.totalSupply() is a public function on the token contract; we can get its address via c.data().borrowAToken but DataView returns the token contract instance which we can call totalSupply() on.

    // run takes two bytes arrays to include inside the multicall array.
    function run(bytes calldata a, bytes calldata b) public payable {
        // read before supplies
        // get DataView to obtain borrowAToken and debtToken addresses
        (uint256 nextDebtPos, uint256 nextCreditPos) = c.getPositionsCount(); // just to interact
